The netplay thread's wait for the socket is at most 2 ms in a match (patches/net-0017).
The console's network.csv (net_diag) showed, in every halt of the 2026-11-26 games, one enet_host_service call lasting
as long as the opponent's silence (401 ms against a 426 ms gap, 227/229, 351/355, ...) with our queued inputs piling up
behind it (queue_max 25, 13, 20): the game queues an input and wakes the netplay thread with a datagram to its own socket
(WakerFunc), and on the console that wake-up did not end the wait. Our inputs then left only when the opponent's next
packet arrived, so the opponent ran out of ours as well and each halt lasted about twice as long. The SD card write of
network.csv was not in that time (the netplay thread wrote it after the service call; net-0016 moved it out of matches).
- In a match the service waits at most kMatchServiceMs (2 ms): what the game queued leaves within that, wake-up or not.
  Outside a match, 250 ms as before. MELEE_NET_SERVICE_MS=N (tests) sets the match value.
- The wake-ups are counted: network.csv columns wakes_sent, wakes_seen, wake_send_max_us; the late-input line says how many
  were sent and how many reached the wait while that input waited; a line at the game's end gives the match's totals; the
  network.csv write logs how long it took (its own thread, after the match).
- MELEE_NET_TEST_NO_WAKE=1 (tests only) sends no wake-ups, as on the console.
Applied by ../net_sources.sh after net-0016.
